#
# chrony.conf — stratum-1 from the Precision Clock Mk IV (via pccd)
#
# Layout: PCC steers; an internet pool is fallback + falseticker jury; the LAN
# is served. Copy the parts you need into your existing chrony.conf — on macOS
# (Homebrew) /opt/homebrew/etc/chrony.conf, on Linux /etc/chrony/chrony.conf.
# Requires pccd running with -s /var/run/chrony.pcc.sock (see README.md).
#

# ---- time sources -----------------------------------------------------------

# The clock. pccd feeds SOF-anchored, prefiltered samples over the SOCK
# refclock (one per 8 s by default; each is already a trimmed mean of 8, ~3 us
# std dev). poll 4 = one measurement per 16 s; filter 8 = median over ~1 min.
# Tuned for THERMAL CHASE, not sample noise: the host's own oscillator moves
# whole ppm per hour under CPU load, and a slow poll leaves the system clock
# free-running between corrections (measured: poll 6 / filter 128 gave 5 us RMS
# idle but 50-66 us RMS during builds; the reference itself is 3 us, so faster
# polling costs nothing). prefer = while PCC is healthy it steers alone.
# Linux note: the arrival-timestamp path is ms-class — use precision 1e-2 there.
refclock SOCK /var/run/chrony.pcc.sock refid PCC precision 1e-4 poll 4 filter 8 prefer

# Fallback + sanity jury. Normally outvoted by 'prefer', but these (a) take over
# seamlessly if the clock is unplugged or pccd stops, (b) expose a malfunctioning
# refclock as a falseticker instead of blindly following it, (c) give
# `chronyc sources` a live cross-check of PCC against world time.
# Replace with a pool near you (e.g. pool.ntp.org, or your ISP's servers).
pool pool.ntp.org iburst maxsources 4

# ---- steering behaviour -----------------------------------------------------

# Correction pacing. 10 balances flat idle tracking against thermal transients:
# 30 was flatter when idle but stretched the response to a ppm-scale frequency
# ramp (a compile job heating the die) into minutes of 100 us-class offset.
# The reference is clean enough (~3 us) that faster correction adds no jitter.
corrtimeratio 10

# Step only at startup (first 3 updates, >100 ms); slew only thereafter.
makestep 0.1 3

# Discard clearly-broken measurements.
maxupdateskew 100.0

# Smear a leap second rather than stepping it into the LAN.
leapsecmode slew

# Learned frequency survives restarts (faster re-convergence after the poll-6
# selection window, which is ~4 min).
driftfile /var/db/chrony/chrony.drift

# ---- serving the LAN --------------------------------------------------------

# Keep answering (at stratum 10) while unsynchronised — e.g. during the
# post-restart selection window — so clients never lose the server entirely.
local stratum 10

# Open the server to your local networks (trim to the ones you actually use).
allow 10/8
allow 192.168/16
allow 172.16/12

# Light abuse protection for the open LAN service.
ratelimit interval 1 burst 16

# ---- housekeeping -----------------------------------------------------------

# cmdmon socket for chronyc.
bindcmdaddress /var/run/chrony/chronyd.sock

# 'refclocks' logs every PCC sample — the file to tune the prefilter against.
log tracking measurements statistics refclocks
logdir /var/log/chrony

dumponexit
dumpdir /var/db/chrony

# Note in syslog any correction over 0.5 s (should never fire in steady state).
logchange 0.5

# Sync the RTC periodically (inert on macOS, harmless).
rtcsync
